{"causes": ["The assassination of Yazdegerd I in 420 CE created a power vacuum and succession crisis within the Sasanian Empire.", "Shapur IV's prior appointment as King of Sasanian Armenia from 415 CE gave him a regional power base and experience in governance.", "The ongoing political rivalries between the Sasanian monarchy, powerful aristocratic families, and the Zoroastrian clergy fueled dynastic instability."], "effects": ["Shapur IV's assassination in 420 CE led to a brief but intense period of internal Sasanian dynastic conflict.", "His death paved the way for the ascension of his younger brother, Bahram V, after significant negotiation with the nobility.", "The instability surrounding Shapur IV's reign further highlighted the diminished power of the monarch relative to the Sasanian aristocracy and clergy."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "yazdegerd-i", "sourceName": "Yazdegerd I", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "shapur-iv", "targetName": "Shapur IV", "context": "Yazdegerd I, as Shapur IV's father, appointed him King of Sasanian Armenia, directly shaping his early political career."}, {"sourceSlug": "shapur-iv", "sourceName": "Shapur IV", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ctesiphon", "targetName": "Ctesiphon", "context": "Shapur IV briefly reigned as Sasanian Shahanshah from Ctesiphon in 420 CE, the imperial capital."}, {"sourceSlug": "shapur-iv", "sourceName": "Shapur IV", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "yazdegerd-i", "targetName": "Yazdegerd I", "context": "Shapur IV briefly succeeded his father, Yazdegerd I, as Sasanian Shahanshah in 420 CE after his assassination."}, {"sourceSlug": "shapur-iv", "sourceName": "Shapur IV", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "bahram-v", "targetName": "Bahram V", "context": "Shapur IV's assassination in 420 CE directly precipitated the struggle for succession that ultimately led to the coronation of his brother Bahram V."}, {"sourceSlug": "sasanian-empire", "sourceName": "Sasanian Empire", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "shapur-iv", "targetName": "Shapur IV", "context": "Shapur IV was a prince and brief ruler within the geographical and political boundaries of the Sasanian Empire."}], "places": [{"name": "Ctesiphon, Sasanian Empire", "role": "Imperial Capital"}, {"name": "Armenia, Sasanian Empire", "role": "Governorship"}, {"name": "Persis, Sasanian Empire", "role": "Sasanian Heartland"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Shapur IV", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "shapur-iv"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1985912", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shapur_IV"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 4, "significanceNarrative": "Shapur IV's extremely brief reign and swift assassination in 420 CE highlights the intense political instability and power struggles within the Sasanian court following Yazdegerd I's death, directly influencing the contentious succession that brought Bahram V to power.", "significanceCategory": "local"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-24T07:04:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40069994818", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Shapur IV, was king of Sasanian Armenia from 415 to 420, who briefly ruled the Sasanian Empire in 420. The only witnesses to this brief reign are the 5th century Armenian historians Łazar Pʿarpecʿi and Movses Khorenatsʿi, and the Mandaean Book of Kings, in which he appears as "King Shābur, son of Yazdiger."", "newValue": "Shapur IV was a Sasanian prince who served as King of Armenia from 415 to 420 CE, and briefly ascended to the Sasanian imperial throne in 420 CE. His short and tumultuous reign represents a critical, albeit contested, moment in the dynastic struggles that characterized the late Sasanian period, specifically following the death of his father, Yazdegerd I. His brief rule underscores the ongoing tension between the Sasanian monarchy's desire for centralized control and the powerful aristocratic factions, alongside the persistent geopolitical pressures from the Roman Empire and internal religious dynamics.

Born to Yazdegerd I, Shapur was initially appointed by his father to govern Sasanian Armenia, a strategically vital buffer state between the Roman and Sasanian empires. This appointment, occurring around 415 CE, aimed to solidify Sasanian influence and potentially integrate Armenia more fully into the empire, as Yazdegerd I had pursued policies of religious tolerance and rapprochement with Rome. Upon Yazdegerd I's assassination in 420 CE, Shapur swiftly returned to the Sasanian capital, Ctesiphon, to claim the imperial crown, a move supported by some elements of the nobility but opposed by powerful Zoroastrian clergy and other princely factions.

However, Shapur IV's reign as Shahanshah was exceptionally brief, lasting only a few months. He was himself assassinated in 420 CE, likely by the same aristocratic and clerical forces that had opposed his father's policies and now sought to install a more compliant ruler. This swift demise plunged the Sasanian Empire into a period of interregnum and further internal conflict, paving the way for the ascension of his younger brother, Bahram V, only after a period of intense political maneuvering and compromise with the powerful magnates."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-24T07:04:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40069994818", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The assassination of Yazdegerd I in 420 CE created a power vacuum and succession crisis within the Sasanian Empire.", "Shapur IV's prior appointment as King of Sasanian Armenia from 415 CE gave him a regional power base and experience in governance.", "The ongoing political rivalries between the Sasanian monarchy, powerful aristocratic families, and the Zoroastrian clergy fueled dynastic instability."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-24T07:04:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40069994818", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "420 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "420 CE"}], "newValue": ["Shapur IV's assassination in 420 CE led to a brief but intense period of internal Sasanian dynastic conflict.", "His death paved the way for the ascension of his younger brother, Bahram V, after significant negotiation with the nobility.", "T